    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d11">

                <pb facs="0321" n="7" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg7"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.0012/532r</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Belgium</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Whitlock</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d11-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-09-15">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">September 15, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>We have just signed treaties with Great Britain, France, Spain and China.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d11-f2"/> These with the twenty-two previously signed connect
                    this Government with more than two thirds of the population of the globe by
                    peace treaties which provide for investigation in all cases before hostilities
                    can be commenced. Please say to the sovereign to whom you are accredited that
                    this Government will be pleased to make a similar treaty with him.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d11-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d11-f1-content">
                <p>The same to the Ambassadors in Germany, Austria-Hungary, and Russia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d11-f2" n="2" xml:id="d11-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1914#pg_304">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1914, p. 304</ref>; <ref
                        target="frus1915#pg_380">1915, p. 380</ref>; <ref target="frus1914#pg_1082"
                        >1914, p. 1082</ref>; <ref target="frus1915#pg_41">1915, p. 41</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
